# Do Not Push to master/main

Never push code to the `master` or `main` branch unless the user explicitly instructs you to do so in the current request.

## Rules

- Do NOT run `git push origin main`, `git push origin master`, `git push` (when current branch is main/master), or any equivalent command targeting these branches.
- Do NOT force-push to main/master under any circumstance.
- Do NOT merge feature branches into main/master locally and push the result without explicit approval.
- If the user asks you to "push" without specifying a branch and the current branch IS main/master, STOP and ask for confirmation first.
- If work needs to be shared, push to a feature branch instead and (if appropriate) open a PR.

## What counts as "explicit approval"

Only a direct instruction in the current turn like:

- "push to main"
- "push to master"
- "yes, push it to main"

A prior instruction in an earlier session does NOT carry over. Ask again.

## Examples

```bash
# ❌ BAD — pushing to main without explicit ask
git checkout main
git push origin main

# ❌ BAD — force push to main
git push --force origin main

# ✅ GOOD — push to feature branch
git push -u origin feature/my-change

# ✅ GOOD — user said "push to main", so it's allowed
git push origin main
```
